><p:nvPr/></p:nvCxnSpPr><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="381000" y="762000"/><a:ext cx="8153400" cy="0"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="straightConnector1"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln cap="flat" cmpd="thinThick" w="57150"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="2FB0DC"/></a:solidFill><a:prstDash val="solid"/><a:miter lim="800000"/><a:headEnd len="med" w="med" type="none"/><a:tailEnd len="med" w="med" type="none"/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:cxnSp><p:pic><p:nvPicPr><p:cNvPr descr="The top image shows a double-helical DNA strand (labeled lower left). It is oriented horizontally. The sugar backbones are represented by blue ribbonlike structures; the bonded base pairs are represented by yellow “rungs” on the DNA ladder. Below this image, an equilibrium arrow is labeled “annealing” on the left (the “up” side) and “denaturation” on the right (the “down” side). The image below is of partially denatured DNA (labeled lower left). From the right, the yellow bonds have begun to break and the DNA helix has begun to unwind, its respective sides splitting apart from one another. The left end of the DNA strand is intact. Another equilibrium arrow is below this image. The left “up” side is labeled “association of strands by base pairing,” while the right “down” side is labeled “separation of strands.” Below the equilibrium arrow is an image of a completely denatured DNA strand. The two sugar backbones are completely separated and the yellow bonds are completely broke. The DNA helix has completely unzipped. On the left is text reading, “separated strands of DNA in random coils.”" id="469" name="Google Shape;469;p68"/><p:cNvPicPr preferRelativeResize="0"/><p:nvPr/></p:nvPicPr><p:blipFill rotWithShape="1"><a:blip r:embed="rId3"><a:alphaModFix/></a:blip><a:srcRect b="0" l="0" r="0" t="0"/><a:stretch/></p:blipFill><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="4876800" y="990600"/><a:ext cx="4083050" cy="5715000"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln><a:noFill/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:pic></p: